1
00:00:01,017 --> 00:00:05,236
By now, you probably understand
that communication is paramount to

2
00:00:05,236 --> 00:00:09,833
a project successful landing—
it's the lifeblood of the whole project.

3
00:00:09,833 --> 00:00:13,040
Communication starts before
the project even begins and

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:16,590
carries on steadily throughout
the remainder of the project.

5
00:00:16,590 --> 00:00:21,205
In this video, I'm going to explain how
using soft skills like negotiation,

6
00:00:21,205 --> 00:00:23,453
delivering messages with empathy,

7
00:00:23,453 --> 00:00:27,898
and asking questions for clarity helps
to foster and strengthen communication.

8
00:00:27,898 --> 00:00:32,961
We'll also discuss feedback as
a foundation for iterating on a product.

9
00:00:32,961 --> 00:00:37,239
According to the Project Management
Institute, it's been found that most

10
00:00:37,239 --> 00:00:40,971
projects experience a communication
breakdown of some kind,

11
00:00:40,971 --> 00:00:45,244
even though project managers spend
about 90% of their time working on

12
00:00:45,244 --> 00:00:46,653
communication alone.

13
00:00:46,653 --> 00:00:51,722
It's in a project manager's best interest
to communicate tactfully, not only with

14
00:00:51,722 --> 00:00:56,459
the members of their own organization, but
also with customers and vendors.

15
00:00:56,459 --> 00:01:00,055
When done well,
strategic communication with a customer or

16
00:01:00,055 --> 00:01:02,668
client can instill
a sense of confidence

17
00:01:02,668 --> 00:01:07,283
that you're doing a good job and
that you're a trustworthy partner.

18
00:01:07,284 --> 00:01:11,692
So how should a project manager approach
communication with a customer?

19
00:01:11,692 --> 00:01:16,293
Using soft skills like negotiation,
empathetic listening, and trust building,

20
00:01:16,293 --> 00:01:20,259
will help foster a good relationship
between you and your customers,

21
00:01:20,259 --> 00:01:25,391
and a good project manager knows how and
when to apply these skills.

22
00:01:25,391 --> 00:01:29,151
A key practice for negotiation,
empathetic listening, and

23
00:01:29,151 --> 00:01:31,567
trust building is asking questions.

24
00:01:31,567 --> 00:01:35,844
It's important to ask open-ended questions
and actively listen to understand

25
00:01:35,844 --> 00:01:39,160
the customer's current state
versus their desired state

26
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:44,384
and what might help them get from here to
there. If you ask open-ended questions,

27
00:01:44,384 --> 00:01:48,423
you'll find out where you can make
your customers feel more secure.

28
00:01:48,423 --> 00:01:52,433
You'll be able to negotiate to ensure
both of your needs are met, and

29
00:01:52,433 --> 00:01:56,673
you'll build the necessary trust
to have a successful partnership.

30
00:01:56,673 --> 00:02:00,781
High-performing project managers set
clear expectations about when they'll

31
00:02:00,781 --> 00:02:03,402
communicate certain things
to their customers.

32
00:02:03,402 --> 00:02:04,112
For example,

33
00:02:04,112 --> 00:02:08,324
you might want to set an expectation that
you'll provide weekly progress updates

34
00:02:08,324 --> 00:02:10,141
to keep clients informed,

35
00:02:10,141 --> 00:02:14,013
rather than expecting them to
come to you with questions.

36
00:02:14,013 --> 00:02:18,571
When troubleshooting an issue with the
product, it might not be necessary to fill

37
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:21,884
the customer in on an issue
that won't affect the outcome.

38
00:02:21,884 --> 00:02:25,621
Let's say a designer on your team quit and
you had to replace them,

39
00:02:25,621 --> 00:02:27,173
you may be able to replace

40
00:02:27,173 --> 00:02:31,450
that designer with a new one fairly
quickly and not even skip a beat in

41
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,083
your project's progress.

42
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:37,960
You can complete the task at hand without
giving the customer any additional worry.

43
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:43,131
The level of visibility between customers
and clients on a given project may vary,

44
00:02:43,131 --> 00:02:46,750
and you'll have to use your judgment
regarding what's important to communicate

45
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:47,489
to your client.

46
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:51,494
Sometimes, you'll want to tell
your client if there's an issue.

47
00:02:51,494 --> 00:02:53,106
If you reach a point in the project

48
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:56,833
where you can't possibly move forward
without their help and input,

49
00:02:56,833 --> 00:03:00,633
you'll have to communicate the issue
to them calmly and with empathy.

50
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:04,426
Let's put this into Project Plant Pals
context, where we're troubleshooting

51
00:03:04,426 --> 00:03:06,730
an issue with broken planters.

52
00:03:06,730 --> 00:03:10,177
Maybe when we were putting
together our quality standards,

53
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:14,584
we left some room for supplier error and
accounted for some broken planters.

54
00:03:14,584 --> 00:03:19,151
We'll say we figured an acceptable
number of broken planters was two out of

55
00:03:19,151 --> 00:03:19,930
every 50.

56
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:23,676
But let's imagine that the customer
received a shipment and

57
00:03:23,676 --> 00:03:25,814
there were five broken planters.

58
00:03:25,814 --> 00:03:28,846
At that point,
we'll need to meet with the customer and

59
00:03:28,846 --> 00:03:31,051
ask important negotiation questions.

60
00:03:31,051 --> 00:03:35,572
We'll need to decide if the five broken
planters out of every 50 is an acceptable

61
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:36,170
outcome,

62
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:40,001
or we'll need to discuss whether
the customer would consider investing in

63
00:03:40,001 --> 00:03:43,120
a higher tier of planters that
may be less prone to breaking.

64
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:49,299
Presenting a solution like using sturdier
planters will affect their budget, and

65
00:03:49,299 --> 00:03:51,392
they'll need to adjust accordingly.

66
00:03:51,392 --> 00:03:56,548
Is the customer okay with that change?
Would that lead to another trade off?

67
00:03:56,548 --> 00:04:00,309
Keeping in mind that the main goal here
is customer satisfaction throughout

68
00:04:00,309 --> 00:04:01,670
the negotiation process,

69
00:04:01,670 --> 00:04:05,989
you'll want to be considerate of
their feelings and limitations.

70
00:04:05,989 --> 00:04:08,684
You can do this by exhibiting empathy:

71
00:04:08,684 --> 00:04:12,130
understanding their frustrations,
addressing them, and

72
00:04:12,130 --> 00:04:15,313
finding a solution that's beneficial for
both of you.

73
00:04:15,313 --> 00:04:18,756
You may have held a customer-facing
role in the past,

74
00:04:18,756 --> 00:04:21,033
whether that's in a call center,

75
00:04:21,033 --> 00:04:26,616
as a retail associate, as a server in
a restaurant, or any number of positions.

76
00:04:26,616 --> 00:04:29,538
Even if you haven't,
you've probably been an advocate for

77
00:04:29,538 --> 00:04:33,820
yourself while conversing with
a customer service representative.

78
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:34,946
Because of this,

79
00:04:34,946 --> 00:04:39,763
you'll probably have a good idea of
what good customer service looks like.

80
00:04:39,763 --> 00:04:44,603
Good customer service results in choosing
to go back to the same hair salon, bakery,

81
00:04:44,603 --> 00:04:45,607
or coffee shop

82
00:04:45,607 --> 00:04:48,286
because you liked the way
that you were treated and

83
00:04:48,286 --> 00:04:50,980
the service you received
(even if you had an issue),

84
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:55,874
versus choosing not to go back to those
places if you don't receive that level

85
00:04:55,874 --> 00:04:56,485
of care.

86
00:04:56,485 --> 00:04:59,764
Your past experiences have taught
you to manage relationships and

87
00:04:59,764 --> 00:05:02,830
to avoid delivering something
that's low quality.

88
00:05:02,830 --> 00:05:07,733
It doesn't feel great when you're on the
receiving end. In order to yield better

89
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:12,509
results in future projects, it's
necessary to get feedback from customers.

90
00:05:12,509 --> 00:05:16,078
Sometimes, this feedback will come
during the process, and sometimes,

91
00:05:16,078 --> 00:05:18,381
it'll come after the project is completed,

92
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:21,236
depending on how you map it
out in the initiation phase.

93
00:05:21,236 --> 00:05:26,096
The matter of when you receive feedback
may come down to what you actually want

94
00:05:26,096 --> 00:05:28,133
to accomplish in your project.

95
00:05:28,133 --> 00:05:31,341
If your business is launching
an e-commerce site,

96
00:05:31,341 --> 00:05:33,131
you will want user feedback

97
00:05:33,131 --> 00:05:37,425
so you can make adjustments to optimize
the customers' shopping experience.

98
00:05:37,425 --> 00:05:40,501
If your business is an on-demand
cookie delivery service,

99
00:05:40,501 --> 00:05:42,464
you may want to deliver the cookies

100
00:05:42,464 --> 00:05:46,666
and then get user feedback to
know how your customers felt

101
00:05:46,666 --> 00:05:50,353
about the cookies and
the delivery experience as a whole.

102
00:05:50,353 --> 00:05:52,478
User feedback helps to close any gaps and

103
00:05:52,478 --> 00:05:56,739
understanding between the customer's
expectation and the project's needs.

104
00:05:56,739 --> 00:06:01,392
So to summarize very briefly, soft skills
like negotiation, empathetic listening,

105
00:06:01,392 --> 00:06:02,693
and trust-building

106
00:06:02,693 --> 00:06:06,340
will help create a good relationship
between you and your customers,

107
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:11,629
and getting feedback from those customers
will help you to iterate on a product or

108
00:06:11,629 --> 00:06:12,343
service.

109
00:06:12,343 --> 00:06:15,088
That's a lot of information—great job taking it all in!

110
00:06:15,088 --> 00:06:19,020
We'll get a little more specific with user
testing and feedback in the next video.

111
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:20,270
I'll meet you there.
